Two common approaches:

**Token bucket**: bucket holds up to `capacity` tokens, refilled at `rate` tokens/second. Each request consumes one token. If empty, reject.

```cpp
class RateLimiter {
    double tokens, capacity, rate;
    chrono::time_point<chrono::steady_clock> last;
public:
    RateLimiter(double cap, double r)
        : tokens(cap), capacity(cap), rate(r), last(chrono::steady_clock::now()) {}

    bool allow() {
        auto now = chrono::steady_clock::now();
        double elapsed = chrono::duration<double>(now - last).count();
        tokens = min(capacity, tokens + elapsed * rate);
        last = now;
        if (tokens >= 1.0) { tokens--; return true; }
        return false;
    }
};
```

**Sliding window log**: store timestamps of recent requests, reject if count in last window exceeds limit. More accurate but uses more memory.
